# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/004_common_subexpression_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_common_subexpression_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Repeated subexpressions produce identical results whether or not eliminated, at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: the largest signed value
computed is product at 9216. The unsigned seed 3000000000 is inside UINT_MAX and unsigned
arithmetic is modular by definition, so no overflow question arises there either. Division
and modulo use divisor 7 and 7u, never zero and never the INT_MIN over -1 case. No shift
operator appears. The negation -(a * b + c) negates 96, not INT_MIN. The variable branchy is
assigned on both arms of the if, so it is never read uninitialized. No pointer arithmetic,
no aliasing violation, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on relative
addresses.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of a
given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int and
unsigned int are used, both 32 bits on all four targets, and long is never used because
sizeof(long) is 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Plain char is not used, so the
measured plain-char signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64
and RISC-V 64) cannot be observed. Unsigned values are printed with %u. No address, pointer
value, sizeof or _Alignof result is printed. No floating point is used. The four seeds are
volatile so their values are opaque to the constant folder, while the arithmetic derived
from them is pure and is therefore a legitimate common subexpression elimination candidate.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
e1=96
e2=96
e1_eq_e2=1
e3=96 e1_eq_e3=1
product=9216
chain=380
nested=91
uexp1=428571432 uexp2=428571432 equal=1
branchy=96
END
